### 60-Second Answer

Four steps. Run them in order. Skip any one and you risk a ring you regret.

1. **Watch the 360° video** — the bow-tie shadow should cover less than 15% of the face-up width. More than 25% means skip the stone.
2. **Check the L:W ratio** — aim for 1.85–2.05. Below 1.75 looks stubby. Above 2.10 increases bow-tie risk.
3. **Set your color floor by metal** — white gold: G minimum. Yellow or rose gold: J minimum. Lab: D–F standard.
4. **Set your clarity floor at VS2** — VS2 is eye-clean. SI1 only with a confirmed clean video. Never SI2.

The video check is the most important step — and the one most people skip.

---

The [marquise cut diamond](/marquise-cut-diamond) is the most shape-sensitive stone you can buy. More than round. More than oval. More than cushion. A bad cut doesn't just look dull — it looks broken. The bow-tie shadow that runs across the stone's center is the single most common complaint. People who skipped the video check mention it first.

Four steps and two minutes of checking. That's the difference between a ring you love and one you look at sideways for years.

> **"The number one mistake I see is buying on the certificate alone. The certificate tells you what the stone is graded. The video tells you what it looks like. For a marquise, those two things can be very different. A Very Good cut can have a severe bow-tie. An Excellent cut can be nearly bow-tie-free. Watch the video."**
> — Farzana Hasan, diamond expert | [About Farzana](/about-farzana/)

---

## Step 1: Watch the 360° Video First

Every reputable retailer provides a 360° video for each certified stone. This is the most important tool you have.

The bow-tie effect is a dark shadow across the widest point of the marquise. It runs perpendicular to the long axis. Every marquise has one. The question is: how wide and how dark?

Here's how to evaluate what you see:

| What you see in the video | What it means | What to do |
|---|---|---|
| Thin shadow, under 15% of face width | Mild bow-tie | Acceptable — move to Step 2 |
| Shadow covers 15–25% of face width | Moderate bow-tie | Borderline — check at all angles |
| Shadow covers over 25% of face width | Severe bow-tie | Skip this stone |
| Shadow shifts when the camera tilts | Cut geometry issue | Avoid entirely |
| **What this means** | Severity varies per stone | Certificate doesn't capture this | Video check is non-negotiable |

How to do the check: play the video at normal speed, then slow it down. Watch the center of the stone as the camera rotates. The bow-tie appears most clearly at the 12 o'clock and 6 o'clock positions. A wide, dark band that doesn't shrink as the stone turns = severe bow-tie.

Check multiple light positions. A clean video under direct light can still hide a severe bow-tie in room conditions. The best retailer videos rotate through at least three light positions. If the video only shows one angle in direct light, ask for more.

The [bow-tie effect](/marquise-diamond-bow-tie) is not graded on any certificate. [GIA](https://www.gia.edu/) does not score it. [IGI](https://www.igi.org/) does not score it. No lab does. The video is your only tool.

---

## The Bow-Tie Window

The bow-tie window is the acceptable severity range for a marquise diamond in daily wear.

Every [marquise cut diamond](/marquise-cut-diamond) has a bow-tie. The shape's geometry creates a dark zone at the center by design. Two pointed ends, curved girdle, elongated face — that's the cause. What varies is the width and depth of that shadow.

The bow-tie window is the range where severity is livable:

| Severity level | Face-up shadow width | Visible in normal wear | Verdict |
|---|---|---|---|
| Minimal | Under 10% | Barely visible | Excellent — rare at this level |
| Mild | 10–15% | Visible but narrow | Good — acceptable for daily wear |
| Moderate | 15–25% | Noticeable in photos | Borderline — check from all angles |
| Severe | Over 25% | Dominant visual feature | Avoid for a center stone |
| **What this means** | Video reveals the true level | Certificate doesn't grade it | Stay inside the window |

Three factors drive severity. First: cut proportions. Tighter pavilion angles reduce the bow-tie. GIA doesn't publish pavilion angle data for marquise stones the way they do for rounds. The only way to see the result of the cut is the video.

Second: length-to-width ratio. Stones with a ratio above 2.10 tend to have more severe bow-ties. The shadow stretches further across the elongated face. This is why L:W ratio is Step 2 — it directly feeds into bow-tie risk.

Third: depth percentage. A stone with depth below 58% sacrifices bow-tie control for face-up size. The stone looks bigger but the bow-tie gets worse. A depth of 58–63% is the balanced range.

Mild bow-ties are normal. They are not a defect. A stone in the mild-to-minimal range passes the window test. You don't need a bow-tie-free stone — you need one that falls inside the window.

---

## Step 2: Pick the Right Length-to-Width Ratio

The L:W ratio controls how the marquise looks on the hand. It's the second check — after the video.

A 1.75 ratio looks stubby. Almost oval-adjacent. At 2.15, the stone looks needle-thin. The sweet spot is 1.85–2.05. Most people who love how their ring looks are in this range.

The ratio is on the certificate. Find the measurements line: length × width × depth. Divide length by width. You want a number between 1.85 and 2.05.

| L:W ratio | Shape impression | Best for |
|---|---|---|
| Under 1.75 | Stubby, nearly oval | Avoid unless strong preference |
| 1.75–1.84 | Wide marquise | Short fingers wanting more band presence |
| 1.85–2.05 | Classic marquise | The standard range — most hands |
| 2.05–2.15 | Elongated marquise | Long fingers, finger-slimming preference |
| Over 2.15 | Needle-thin | Higher bow-tie risk; fragile tips |
| **What this means** | Ratio shapes the silhouette | Check the certificate first | 1.85–2.05 fits most hands |

Hand size matters here. On a size 5 finger, a 1ct at 1.95 reads very differently than on a size 8. The [marquise diamond length to width ratio](/marquise-diamond-length-to-width-ratio) guide has full finger size comparisons.

One practical note: the L:W ratio is fixed once the stone is cut. You can't resize the stone. Pick a ratio you're sure about before ordering.

---

## The L:W Sweet Spot

The sweet spot is the 1.85–2.05 range. Inside this range, the marquise delivers three things at once. Maximum face-up size per carat. Balanced silhouette. Reduced bow-tie risk.

Below 1.85, the elongated effect fades. You stop reading "marquise" and start reading "wide oval with points." The shape loses its defining characteristic.

Above 2.05, the stone slims down. This works on some longer fingers. But above 2.10, bow-tie risk climbs and the stone can look fragile. The tips become more vulnerable to chipping at very high ratios.

Here's why the sweet spot works for size. A 1ct marquise at a 1.90 ratio measures roughly 11.0mm × 5.8mm. That's 32% more face-up area than a 1ct round brilliant. The marquise's core advantage — more perceived size per carat — comes from this ratio. Step outside the sweet spot and you lose it.

| Marquise ratio | Face-up area (1ct) | Compared to 1ct round | Perceived size advantage |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1.75 | Smaller | Less than 20% more | Low — closer to oval look |
| 1.90 | ~64mm² | ~32% more than round | High — maximum elongation |
| 2.05 | ~63mm² | ~30% more than round | High — still in the window |
| 2.15+ | Varies | Visually thinner | Lower perceived size per carat |
| **What this means** | Sweet spot maximizes size | Round loses the comparison | Stay in 1.85–2.05 for best value |

The marquise earns its reputation for size efficiency inside the sweet spot. That's the practical reason the 1.85–2.05 range isn't just aesthetic — it's structural.

> **"I always ask: hold your hand flat and imagine the stone pointing toward your knuckle. At 1.90 ratio, it fills that space gracefully. Below 1.80, it looks like a wide oval. Above 2.10, it looks like a sliver. The number on the certificate becomes very real when you hold your hand up."**
> — Farzana Hasan, diamond expert | [About Farzana](/about-farzana/)

---

## Step 3: Set Your Color Floor by Metal

Color grade determines how white or warm the stone looks face-up. Metal choice determines which color grades work in your setting.

White gold and platinum reflect cool light through the stone. That masking effect makes lower color grades look whiter than they grade. Yellow and rose gold reflect warm light. That warmth can neutralize the slight yellow tint in lower color grades.

The rule: match your color floor to your metal. Going above the floor wastes budget on a difference you can't see in normal wear.

| Metal | Color floor | Color ceiling | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| 14K or 18K white gold | G | D | I is acceptable; below I shows warmth |
| Platinum | G | D | Same masking effect as white gold |
| 14K yellow gold | J | G | J reads colorless in warm metal |
| 14K rose gold | J | G | Pink tone neutralizes faint warmth |
| Lab diamond, any metal | D–F | D | D–F is the standard lab range |
| **What this means** | Metal locks your floor | Going higher than needed = wasted spend | Match metal first, then pick color |

The color floor is the minimum grade that looks white to the naked eye in your metal. Below the floor, warmth is visible from a normal viewing distance of 12–18 inches.

Going above the floor means paying for a difference you can't see in daily wear. It only shows in a controlled side-by-side comparison. In yellow gold, D color looks identical to H color from across the room.

For side-by-side grade comparisons in white gold vs. yellow gold, see the [marquise diamond color guide](/marquise-diamond-color-guide).

---

## The Color-Metal Lock

Once you pick a metal, your color floor is set. You can always go higher — but the visible result doesn't change past a certain point.

Here's the lock in practice:

**White gold / platinum:** Floor is G. H and I are also acceptable — the cool metal masks slight warmth. Going to D, E, or F costs a premium for a difference that requires a lab comparison to see. The right target is G or H. Past H, you're paying for the certificate number.

**Yellow gold:** Floor is J. J color reads white in yellow gold. H and I are fine too. Going above G in yellow gold gives you no visible return. Color above G in a warm metal is undetectable in normal conditions.

**Rose gold:** Treat it like yellow gold. Floor is J. The pink tone in rose gold absorbs the slight yellow warmth in J–K stones. D or E color in rose gold adds cost with no visual payoff.

**Lab diamonds:** Graded on the same [GIA](https://www.gia.edu/) or [IGI](https://www.igi.org/) scale as natural. The same color-metal rules apply. Lab stones are priced with smaller gaps between grades. Most lab stones in certified inventory start at D–F. That's fine — the price difference between D and G lab is small relative to the total cost.

The lock cuts in both directions. Don't go below the floor — warmth shows. Don't go above H in white gold or G in yellow gold — the premium doesn't show.

For the exact visual difference by grade in each metal, see the [marquise diamond color guide](/marquise-diamond-color-guide).

---

## Step 4: Set Your Clarity Floor

Clarity determines whether you can see inclusions with the naked eye. The floor is VS2.

VS2 clarity marquise diamonds are eye-clean from a normal viewing distance in nearly every case. SI1 may work — but only with a confirmed clean video. SI2 or lower shows inclusions that you will notice once you know where to look.

| Clarity grade | Visible at 12 inches | Video check required | Verdict |
|---|---|---|---|
| VVS1 / VVS2 | Invisible | No | Premium — rarely adds visible value |
| VS1 | Invisible | No | Safe choice |
| VS2 | Invisible in most cases | Optional | The floor — correct for most |
| SI1 | May be visible | Yes — mandatory | Only buy with a clean video |
| SI2 | Often visible | Yes — likely to fail | Avoid for center stone |
| I1 or lower | Visible to naked eye | N/A | Not suitable |
| **What this means** | VS2 is the safe minimum | SI1 needs video proof | Never buy SI1 without watching first |

The marquise brilliant facet pattern (56 facets) scatters light better than step cuts. An SI1 inclusion that would be obvious in an emerald cut may be invisible in a marquise. But "may be invisible" is not good enough without checking.

For SI1 video checks: look for dark spots under the center table facet. Dark inclusions there are most visible. White or feather inclusions near the girdle edge are far less visible. An SI1 with a dark crystal under the table will fail the video test. An SI1 with a small feather near the girdle may pass.

Full detail on which inclusion types affect marquise diamonds most is in the [marquise diamond clarity guide](/marquise-diamond-clarity-guide).

---

## The Clarity Floor Rule

The clarity floor rule: buy VS2, verify SI1, skip SI2.

Here's why VS2 is the floor and not VS1 or VVS2:

The price curve. At 0.75ct natural, the jump from SI1 to VS2 costs $100–$200. The jump from VS2 to VS1 costs $100–$300. The jump from VS1 to VVS2 costs $300–$600. Each step above VS2 adds money for a difference that disappears at normal viewing distance.

VS2 sits at the inflection point. It's the last grade that is reliably eye-clean without needing video confirmation. VVS and VS1 are also eye-clean — but they cost more. SI1 may be eye-clean — but you need the video to know. VS2 gives you reliable eye-clean clarity without the premium.

One exception: a large table facet or step-cut component needs VS1. Standard brilliant-cut marquise? VS2 is correct.

What to watch for in an SI1 video: slow the video to half speed. Look at the center of the stone from directly above. Dark crystals or carbon spots under the table are the inclusions to avoid. They appear as dark dots or shadows that don't move with the stone rotation. Avoid any SI1 where you can spot an inclusion in the first 10 seconds of watching.

![Marquise diamond face-up size and L:W ratio comparison — how ratio affects perceived size across carat weights](/images/marquise-diamond-maximum-perceived-size-editorial-infographic.jpg)

