## All 4 Steps — Side-by-Side

| Step | What to check | Pass | Fail — find another stone |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 — Video | Bow-tie shadow width | Under 15% of face width | Over 25% — skip |
| 2 — L:W ratio | Certificate measurements | 1.85–2.05 | Under 1.75 or over 2.15 |
| 3 — Color | Grade matched to metal | G+ for WG/plat · J+ for YG/RG | Below color floor for chosen metal |
| 4 — Clarity | Grade + video for SI1 | VS2+; or SI1 with clean video | SI2 or lower; SI1 without video |
| **What this means** | Run all 4 before ordering | 4 passes = ready to buy | Any fail = move to next stone |

Run them in this order. Step 1 eliminates the most stones fast. Step 2 eliminates the next group. Steps 3 and 4 fine-tune within what remains.

If a stone passes all four, it's ready. If it fails any one, move on. There are thousands of certified marquise diamonds in active inventory. You don't need to compromise on any step.

---

## How to Read a GIA Certificate for a Marquise

A [GIA](https://www.gia.edu/) certificate for a marquise contains specific fields. Here's what matters:

**Shape and cutting style:** Should read "Marquise Brilliant." If it says "Marquise Modified Brilliant," the facet pattern is nonstandard. Watch the video more carefully.

**Measurements:** Three numbers — length × width × depth. Divide length by width for the L:W ratio. This is your Step 2 input.

**Carat weight:** The actual weight. A 1.00ct marquise looks larger face-up than a 1.00ct round because of the elongated shape. This is the size efficiency advantage.

**Color grade:** D through Z. D–F is colorless. G–J is near-colorless. Apply Step 3 here.

**Clarity grade:** FL through I3. Apply Step 4 here.

**Cut grade:** GIA grades round brilliant cut as Excellent/Very Good/Good. For marquise, GIA does not assign a formal cut grade. The certificate shows polish and symmetry grades — those are not the same thing. Two marquise stones both graded "Excellent" for polish can have completely different bow-tie severity. This is the core reason Step 1 is the video, not the certificate.

**Inclusions plot:** The diagram on the right side shows where inclusions sit. For SI1 stones, check whether the inclusion symbols land under the table (center) or near the girdle (edge). Center inclusions are more visible.

**Fluorescence:** Strong fluorescence in D–F color stones can cause a hazy look in sunlight. Faint or none is the safe default for colorless grades. For G–I color, medium blue fluorescence can improve face-up whiteness under UV. The fluorescence entry is at the bottom of the grading report.

For [IGI](https://www.igi.org/)-certified lab diamonds, the certificate fields are identical. Read them the same way.

---

## Lab Diamonds: Does the Buying Process Change?

The same four steps apply to lab-grown marquise diamonds. Watch the video. Check the L:W ratio. Match color to metal. Set clarity at VS2.

Two things change:

**The certification.** Most lab marquise stones are [IGI](https://www.igi.org/)-certified. IGI is the industry standard for lab stones. [GIA](https://www.gia.edu/) also certifies lab diamonds now. Both are reliable. The certificate fields are the same.

**The price math.** Lab diamonds cost 60–80% less than natural for the same grade. That means you can reach higher grades for the same budget. At $3,000, you choose between a 0.50ct natural G VS2 or a 1ct lab D VVS2. The lab option delivers more grade and more size.

At $5,000, a 1.5ct D IF lab stone is real. A natural 1ct I VS2 is also real. Both pass all four steps. The buying process is identical. The difference is in size, grade level, and origin story.

For the full lab vs. natural comparison at this shape, see [Marquise Natural vs Lab](/marquise-natural-vs-lab).

---

## Stone First, Then Setting

A common mistake: pick the setting style first, then find a stone to fit the remaining budget. This works for rounds. For a marquise, it creates problems.

Marquise settings vary more than round settings. Prong count, tip protection, and head size all interact with the stone's visual proportions. A 1ct marquise at 1.90 in a V-tip solitaire reads differently. A six-prong cathedral head changes the visual balance entirely.

The right order: find a stone that passes all four steps first. Then choose a setting that fits the stone and your metal preference.

Why this matters: you can't set a setting budget until the stone cost is locked. A [GIA](https://www.gia.edu/)-certified natural 1ct at $4,049 leaves $951 for a setting in a $5,000 budget. An IGI 1.5ct lab at $2,682 leaves $2,318. Stone cost determines setting range.

Set your total budget. Run the four steps. Find a stone that passes. Choose the setting from what's left. That sequence produces the right ring. The reverse produces compromise.

For setting styles by budget, see the [marquise solitaire](/marquise-solitaire-engagement-ring), [marquise three stone](/marquise-three-stone-engagement-ring), and [marquise pavé](/marquise-pave-engagement-ring) guides.

