## Common Mistakes and How to Avoid Them

Most marquise buying mistakes come down to one of four patterns:

**Mistake 1: Buying on the certificate without watching the video.** The certificate is a data document. It tells you the grade. The video tells you the result. For a marquise, the grade and the visual result can be very different. A GIA Very Good polish stone can have a severe bow-tie. Watching the video takes two minutes. Not watching can cost you thousands in regret.

**Mistake 2: Ignoring the L:W ratio.** Most shoppers focus on carat, color, and clarity. The L:W ratio is the fourth dimension of the shape — and it determines whether the stone looks elegant or awkward on the hand. A ratio outside 1.85–2.05 changes the ring's entire visual character. Check the certificate measurements before you look at anything else.

**Mistake 3: Going above the color floor.** D color in yellow gold. E color in rose gold. These are real purchases that happen frequently. The extra cost is real. The visual benefit is not. Set your metal first. Set your color floor from the lock table. Stop there.

**Mistake 4: Skipping down to SI1 without the video.** Some shoppers drop to SI1 or SI2 to save money, then discover an inclusion that bothers them in photos. VS2 is the safe floor for a reason. If you need to go to SI1 for budget reasons, watch the video three times from different playback speeds before committing.

| Mistake | Why it happens | How to avoid it |
|---|---|---|
| Buying without video | Certificate looks complete | Watch video before any other check |
| Wrong L:W ratio | Focus on 4 Cs only | Divide certificate measurements first |
| Color above the floor | Aiming for highest grade | Match color to metal, stop at floor |
| SI1 without video | Budget pressure | VS2 default; SI1 requires clean video |
| **What this means** | All four are preventable | Use the 4-step sequence | Two minutes of checking prevents all four |

---

## How to Order Without Overpaying

Three checks before you click buy.

**1. Confirm the stone is still in stock.** Certified inventory moves. Stone IDs change availability daily. Always click the stone link first. Confirm it's active. Then go to the setting.

**2. Check the depth percentage.** Find it on the certificate under "Depth %". The ideal range is 58–63%. Below 58% sacrifices bow-tie control for face-up size. Above 65% retains light well but the stone looks smaller than its carat weight.

**3. Verify the tip protection in the setting.** Marquise tips chip. V-tip prongs (also called V-prongs or claw prongs) are mandatory at both pointed ends. Any setting without V-tips puts the stone at risk. Check the setting description before pairing.

One sizing note: order half a size up if you're between sizes. Resizing a solitaire up or down by one full size costs $30–$100. A channel or pavé band is harder to resize. A marquise solitaire can usually be adjusted by up to two sizes. Check with the retailer first.

> **"People ask me what the biggest buying regret is at this shape. It's always one of two things: either they didn't watch the video and the bow-tie is bad, or they ordered the wrong L:W ratio because they didn't check the certificate measurements. Both are fixable before purchase. Neither is fixable after."**
> — Farzana Hasan, diamond expert | [About Farzana](/about-farzana/)

