## Frequently Asked Questions

### What is the most important thing to check when buying a marquise diamond?

The 360° video. The bow-tie effect varies by individual stone and is not graded on the [GIA](https://www.gia.edu/) certificate. A Very Good cut can have a severe bow-tie. An Excellent cut can have a mild one. The video is the only way to tell. Watch it before reading anything else on the listing.

### What L:W ratio should I look for in a marquise diamond?

1.85–2.05 is the standard range. Below 1.75 looks stubby. Above 2.10 increases bow-tie risk and makes the stone look needle-thin. Find the ratio by dividing the length measurement by the width measurement on the certificate.

### What color grade is best for a marquise diamond in white gold?

G is the practical floor. G reads colorless in white gold and platinum. H and I are also acceptable — the cool metal masks slight warmth. D, E, or F cost more for a difference that requires a controlled comparison to detect.

### What color grade is best for a marquise diamond in yellow gold?

J is the practical floor. Yellow gold reflects warm light that neutralizes the faint tint in J–K color stones. G is fine but pays a premium you can't see in daily wear. H and I both work. J is the value target for yellow and rose gold.

### Is VS2 clarity good enough for a marquise diamond?

Yes. VS2 is eye-clean from a normal viewing distance in a marquise brilliant. The 56-facet pattern scatters light and conceals small inclusions. SI1 can also work — but only after confirming no visible inclusions under the table on video. VS2 is the safe floor where video-checking becomes optional.

### Can you see a bow-tie in an SI1 clarity stone?

A bow-tie is a cut issue, not a clarity issue. SI1 refers to inclusions — small internal features. The bow-tie is a dark shadow caused by the stone's geometry. They are separate. A VS1 stone can have a severe bow-tie. An SI1 stone can have a mild one. Always check both: clarity on the certificate, bow-tie in the video.

### How does GIA grade a marquise cut?

[GIA](https://www.gia.edu/) does not assign a formal cut grade to marquise diamonds. For round brilliants, GIA grades Excellent/Very Good/Good/Fair/Poor. For marquise, GIA only grades polish and symmetry. There is no equivalent overall cut grade. The bow-tie severity — a direct result of cut proportions — is not captured anywhere on the certificate. The video is the only way to assess it.

### What is the difference between IGI and GIA for marquise diamonds?

[GIA](https://www.gia.edu/) is the most widely accepted standard for natural diamonds. It grades conservatively. [IGI](https://www.igi.org/) is the standard for lab-grown diamonds and is fully accepted by the trade. For natural marquise stones, GIA certification gives the most reliable resale documentation. For lab stones, IGI is appropriate and universally recognized.

### Does the marquise shape hide inclusions well?

Better than step cuts (emerald, asscher), yes. The brilliant facet pattern scatters light and conceals inclusions more effectively than a step-cut's open table. It doesn't hide inclusions as well as a round brilliant. VS2 is still the safe floor. The shape's scattering is not a reason to skip VS2 without checking the video first.

### What depth percentage should I look for?

58–63% is the target range. Below 58%, the stone trades depth for face-up size — looks large but may lose brilliance. Above 65%, the stone keeps light well but looks smaller than its carat weight suggests face-up. Find the depth percentage on the certificate directly below the measurements.

### What prong style is mandatory for a marquise diamond?

V-tip prongs at both pointed ends. The marquise's pointed tips chip easily without metal protection. V-tip prongs (also called V-prongs or claw tips) wrap around each point. Beyond tip protection, prong style is aesthetic. Six-prong settings spread holding force around the girdle. Four-prong allows more light. Bezel settings fully enclose the stone — maximum protection, minimum brilliance. V-tip six-prong or V-tip four-prong are the most practical for daily wear.

### Is strong fluorescence bad for a marquise diamond?

Not automatically. Strong fluorescence in D–F color stones can cause a hazy look in sunlight. Check the video in natural light if fluorescence is listed as Strong or Very Strong. For G–I color stones, medium blue fluorescence can improve face-up whiteness. Faint or none is the safest default. The fluorescence strength is listed on the [GIA](https://www.gia.edu/) grading report.

### How do I know if an SI1 marquise is eye-clean from the video?

Play the video at half speed. Watch the center of the stone from directly above. Dark crystals or carbon spots appear as fixed dark marks. They don't move when the stone rotates. Spot one in the first 10 seconds at half speed — it will show in daily wear. Can't find it after 30 seconds of looking? It's likely eye-clean. Err on VS2 if there's any doubt.

### Should I buy the stone or the setting first?

The stone first, always. The stone cost sets your remaining setting budget. Committing to a $2,000 setting before knowing the stone cost builds the plan backward. Find a stone that passes all four steps. Lock in the stone price. Choose the setting from what's left in your total budget.
